.\" tafas(1) — manual page. Kept in the repository and installed by the .deb, the
.\" .rpm, the Homebrew formula and the release archives. `tests/packaging.rs`
.\" fails when an option in `tafas --help` is missing here, so this page cannot
.\" drift into describing a different program.
.TH TAFAS 1 "2026-11-04" "tafas 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tafas \- capture a video from YouTube, TikTok or X as one playable MP4
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tafas
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIURL\fR...
.br
.B tafas
\fB\-\-list\-formats\fR | \fB\-\-capabilities\fR | \fB\-\-info\fR |
\fB\-\-completions\fR \fI SHELL\fR | \fB\-\-man\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tafas
reads a page, picks a stream, downloads it and writes one file. When a site
serves picture and sound separately, as YouTube does above 720p,
.B tafas
joins them into one MP4 without re-encoding: the sample tables are copied and
the container index is rebuilt, so the output holds the frames the site served.
Nothing is transcoded, no quality setting exists, and no external program is
run.
.PP
Every URL is one item. Items are independent: one failure does not stop the
others, and the exit code says whether any failed. The written path goes to
stdout, one per line; progress, notes, warnings and errors go to stderr. A
closed stdout pipe ends the run quietly and successfully.
.PP
A file is written through a temporary
.I <target>.part
beside the destination and renamed into place only after its byte count has been
checked \- and, for a remuxed file, after its structure has been re-read from
disk. An interrupted run leaves the
.I .part
file behind; the next run continues it when the server supports range requests.
.SH SUPPORTED SITES
.TP
.B YouTube
Watch pages, \fByoutu.be\fR short links, \fB/shorts/\fR, \fB/embed/\fR and
\fB/live/\fR URLs. The watch page's inlined player response is read first; when
that yields nothing, the InnerTube JSON API is asked through a fixed chain of
clients (\fBandroid_vr\fR, \fBios\fR, \fBandroid\fR, the embedded TV client,
\fBweb\fR). Formats that arrive signature-protected are dropped rather than
deciphered, and a page that demands a sign-in check is a refusal.
.TP
.B TikTok
Video and photo posts, including \fBvm.tiktok.com\fR and \fBvt.tiktok.com\fR
short links. The page's rehydration document is read first, the mobile feed
second. The version without a watermark is preferred when the page offers both.
.TP
.B X
Posts with video, animated GIF or photos, through the syndication endpoint. A
post that offers only an HLS playlist is fetched segment by segment, demuxed and
written as an MP4 in process; a progressive MP4 is preferred when both exist.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Destination
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-output \fIPATH\fR
Output file, or directory when more than one URL is given. A trailing slash
forces the directory reading. \fB\-\fR writes a single stream to stdout, which
is refused for anything that needs a remux.
.TP
.B \-\-name \fINAME\fR
Use this name stem instead of \fI<title> [<id>]\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Replace an existing file. Without it an existing destination is an error, and
nothing is fetched.
.TP
.B \-\-write\-info\-json
Write \fI<name>.info.json\fR beside each file: the page, the title, the streams
the page offered, which one was taken, and the files that were written. Media
URLs are deliberately left out \— a signed URL is a credential that expires
within hours, and a file that outlives it should not carry one.
.SS Selection
.TP
.B \-Q, \-\-quality \fIQUALITY\fR
\fBbest\fR (default), \fBworst\fR, or a height such as \fB720p\fR. A height is a
ceiling: when nothing that small exists, the smallest available stream is taken.
.TP
.B \-\-audio\-only
Take the audio stream alone, as \fBm4a\fR when the site offers one.
.TP
.B \-\-format \fIID\fR
Fetch exactly this stream, using an id printed by \fB\-\-list\-formats\fR.
Requires one URL.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mux
Keep picture and sound in separate files; never remux.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-formats
Print every stream of each URL and stop. Nothing is downloaded.
.SS Transfer
.TP
.B \-\-no\-resume
Do not continue an interrupted download. By default a \fI.part\fR file is
continued with a range request.
.TP
.B \-\-retries \fIN\fR
Retries for one failed request (default 3). Transport errors, \fB429\fR and
\fB5xx\fR are retried with a growing delay; other \fB4xx\fR responses are not.
.TP
.B \-\-timeout \fISECS\fR
Deadline for one metadata request (default 60). A media transfer has no total
deadline.
.TP
.B \-j, \-\-jobs \fIN\fR
How many URLs to work on at once (default 1; \fB0\fR means four).
.TP
.B \-\-cookies \fIFILE\fR
Send the cookies in a Netscape cookie file, in the format curl writes and every
browser exports. The file is read once at startup, sent only to the hosts its
domains match, never written back and never logged. It is the only credential
this program accepts, and it stores nothing between runs.
.TP
.B \-\-playlist\-items \fIITEMS\fR
Take only these entries of a list: \fB3\fR, \fB1\-5\fR, \fB2\-\fR or any
comma\-separated mix, counted from one. Today the lists are the segment lists of
an HLS playlist; the item is a segment, and the file is assembled from the ones
named. A selection that matches nothing is a refusal, not an empty file. A
selection that does not begin on a keyframe produces a warning, because the
first frames are then undecodable.
.SS Output
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be fetched, in which shape, and where, then stop.
.TP
.B \-\-json
One JSON object per item on stdout. The schema is described in \fIdocs/cli.md\fR.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Errors only. The written path is still printed to stdout, because it is data.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-progress
Never draw the progress line.
.TP
.B \-\-color \fIWHEN\fR
\fBauto\fR (default), \fBalways\fR, \fBnever\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Same as \fB\-\-color never\fR.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Log requests and decisions on stderr. Repeatable.
.SS Informational
.TP
.B \-\-completions \fISHELL\fR
Print a completion script for bash, zsh, fish, powershell or elvish, generated
from the live parser.
.TP
.B \-\-man
Print this manual page in groff source and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-capabilities
Print a JSON description of what this build can do. Read-only: it makes no
request and touches no file.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-info
Print the version, the supported sites and the standing defaults.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every URL succeeded, or the answer was informational.
.TP
.B 1
At least one URL failed; the others were still processed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage. Nothing was requested.
.TP
.B 3
A fatal error: the run could not be completed.
.TP
.B 4
A written file failed verification. This is a bug in \fBtafas\fR.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted. A \fI.part\fR file may remain; the next run continues it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR, CLICOLOR, CLICOLOR_FORCE, TERM
Decide whether colour is used when \fB\-\-color auto\fR is in effect, in that
order of precedence. \fBTERM=dumb\fR disables colour.
.TP
.B HTTPS_PROXY, HTTP_PROXY, NO_PROXY
Honoured by the HTTP client.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <target>.part
A transfer in progress. Removed by a successful run.
.TP
.I <target>.video.part, <target>.audio.part
The two halves of a remux, present only while it is running or after a failure.
.TP
.I <target stem>.info.json
Written only with \fB\-\-write\-info\-json\fR, and only after the file it
describes has been renamed into place.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1)
.SH BUGS
Signature-protected YouTube formats and fMP4 HLS playlists are refused rather
than worked around; \fIdocs/extractors.md\fR states exactly what is and is not
supported. Report bugs at https://github.com/RUdolf1517/tafas/issues.
